{"conversation_id": "conv_001", "status": "started", "response": "Imagine you’re studying for a math test, but instead of learning how to do many kinds of problems, you just memorize the exact answers to the 20 questions in your homework. You get 100% on the homework, because you’ve seen those questions before. But when the test has new questions that look a little different, you get confused and do badly. That’s like overfitting in machine learning.

In machine learning, we give a computer many examples (data) so it can learn patterns and make predictions. Overfitting happens when the computer memorizes the training examples too closely, including all the little quirks and “noise” that don’t really matter. It looks amazing on the training data, but when it sees new data, it makes lots of mistakes because it never really understood the general idea.

To avoid overfitting, we try to help the model learn the big, important patterns instead of every tiny detail. We do things like: use more data, keep the model simpler, and test it on separate data it hasn’t seen before (a “validation” or “test” set). If it does well on both the training data and the new data, then it has learned to generalize instead of overfit.", "tokens_used": {"input": 1117, "output": 260, "cached": 0}}
